::SPEECH_U06_L01_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L01_GuidedPractice_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which message best fits a professional audience?
{
~A. Use casual wording that does not fit the setting#This choice is incomplete because it does not match the professional purpose, audience, or communication setting.
=B. Could you please clarify which shift I should report to on Friday?#Correct. This choice supports professional communication by matching purpose, audience, tone, listening, and collaboration expectations.
~C. Ignore the person receiving the message#This choice reflects a misconception. Recheck audience, tone, listening, or collaboration expectations.
~D. Use an accusatory tone when asking for help#This choice weakens communication because it ignores how the listener or group will understand the message.
}
::SPEECH_U06_L01_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_L01_GuidedPractice_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which choice best identifies purpose and audience?
{
~A. Use casual wording that does not fit the setting#This choice is incomplete because it does not match the professional purpose, audience, or communication setting.
~B. Make the message vague and hard to answer#This choice may sound related, but it does not fully support respectful professional communication or discussion skill.
=C. Purpose is the reason for communicating and audience is who receives the message#Correct. This choice supports professional communication by matching purpose, audience, tone, listening, and collaboration expectations.
~D. Use an accusatory tone when asking for help#This choice weakens communication because it ignores how the listener or group will understand the message.
}
